WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.380
DEAD RIVALRY:
Hundreds of millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:06.120
of people are ditching Android. Once upon a&nbsp;
time, the rivalry between Android and iPhone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:12.000
was one of the biggest in the world. It was as&nbsp;
divisive and alienating as politics, religion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.040
and whether you eat the frosting in an Oreo&nbsp;
first. The iPhoners were seen as snobs who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.020
worshipped Steve Jobs and were willing to pay up&nbsp;
any price. Meanwhile, the Androiders were seen as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:27.600
the geeks who just didn’t quite understand the&nbsp;
elegance of the iPhone. For the longest time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:34.080
it seemed like this battle had been settled the&nbsp;
same way as the PC vs Mac battle. The PC was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.060
choice for the overwhelming majority while the&nbsp;
Mac was the choice for a few creative types,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.820
coders, and of course snobs. But, it’s not&nbsp;
quite playing out the same way within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:50.400
smartphone space. In fact, the iPhone has&nbsp;
not only been gaining on Android but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.440
has straight up overtaken Android in terms of&nbsp;
popularity within the US. Things haven’t gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.940
that far internationally but the iPhone&nbsp;
is still very much gaining on Android&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:06.300
outside of the US as well. In fact, within just&nbsp;
the last 5 years, Android’s global market share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:16.320
has fallen 8% from 77 down to 69. That might&nbsp;
not sound like a lot but keep in mind, virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:23.520
everyone in the entire world has a smartphone.&nbsp;
We’re talking about 6.92 billion people. So an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:30.000
8% loss in marketshare translates to over half&nbsp;
a billion people switching teams. Another decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.400
of that and Android will no longer control the&nbsp;
majority. And honestly, looking past the numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:42.180
it seems that this shift is already underway. Back&nbsp;
in day, it was the iPhone users that were seen as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.700
the outsiders, the select minority of Apple&nbsp;
sheep. But, today, it’s usually the Android&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:53.700
users that are seen as the outsiders. A green text&nbsp;
bubble is a legitimate reason to not date someone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:59.940
facetime is a must have, and even flagship apps&nbsp;
like Instagram and Snapchat are very much biased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:06.060
towards iPhone as they work way better on iPhone.&nbsp;
This is especially true for younger generations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:13.500
aka the future of the smartphone market, of whom&nbsp;
79% prefer iPhone. Hearing that, it seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:19.740
the demise of Android is almost inevitable,&nbsp;
so what happened to the king of smartphones?

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:28.980
THE CASE FOR ANDROID:
To answer what happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.420
the Android community, we first have to take&nbsp;
a look back at what gave rise to the Android&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.920
community in the first palace and for&nbsp;
that, we have to go all the way back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:44.040
October of 2003. This is when four friends&nbsp;
named Andy Rubin, Rich Miner, Nick Sears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:49.980
and Chris White would create Android but it was&nbsp;
nothing like what we know today. In fact, Android&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:55.500
wasn’t even meant for smartphones as smartphones&nbsp;
weren’t even a thing. Android was actually trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.540
to create better software for digital cameras&nbsp;
but they quickly figured out that this was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.760
rather niche industry with little money. So, out&nbsp;
of desperation, they would pivot to the mobile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.920
phone industry which got off to a surprisingly&nbsp;
strong start. In fact, they would end up having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:16.140
conversations talks with Samsung and HTC. And&nbsp;
these negotiations would catch the attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.940
of an up and coming startup named Google, you&nbsp;
might’ve heard of them. Google would go ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:27.060
and buyout the company for a mere $50 million.&nbsp;
With Google’s backing, Android no longer had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:33.000
worry about funding but they still didn’t have a&nbsp;
winning product in their hands. In fact, Android&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.220
was initially being developed for blackberry like&nbsp;
devices with physical keyboards and no touchscreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.960
functionality. It wasn’t until the launch of the&nbsp;
iPhone that Google went back to the drawing board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.180
and focused on a touch screen based experience.&nbsp;
Even then though, they wanted some fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:53.460
functions to be represented by physical buttons.&nbsp;
And that’s why all the early Androids came with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:59.640
the classic 3 buttons at the bottom. This blatant&nbsp;
copying made Steve Jobs furious as he would vow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:05.580
to destroy Android but that’s a wholenother story.&nbsp;
The fact was that while Android had a rocky start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:12.180
the 2010s would be a rocketship as their&nbsp;
marketshare exploded from some 20% to nearly 70%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:19.712
This explosion can be attributed to 3 main factors&nbsp;
starting with price. This one is pretty self&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.712 --> 00:04:25.200
explanatory. Androids were in general far cheaper&nbsp;
than iPhones. The cheapest Androids started at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:31.680
just $100 to $200 making them far more accessible&nbsp;
than the $600 to $700 iPhones especially in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.240
developing countries. But, don’t let this cheap&nbsp;
price fool you into thinking that Androids weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:42.060
capable. In fact, Androids often had way better&nbsp;
specs than iPhones for half the price which brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:47.700
us into our second point for Android: performance.&nbsp;
Before Samsung started cheating on benchmarks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.740
and got banned from Geekbench, they used to&nbsp;
legitimately be the king of performance and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:58.020
it’s no wonder why. Just take a look at the specs&nbsp;
of the iPhone 6 and the Galaxy S6 for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:04.260
The Samsung had a 40% larger battery, a nearly&nbsp;
2 times higher pixel density, 3 times more ram,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:10.320
a 3.5 times higher resolution, over 5 times&nbsp;
the CPU speed, and a camera with double the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.240
megapixels. So, for people who made their choice&nbsp;
based on the spec sheet and price to performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:21.300
Android was the clear way to go. And speaking&nbsp;
of choices, there were simply far more choices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:26.880
with Android. You could go with Samsung,&nbsp;
Google, HTC, OnePlus, Oppo, Xiaomi, Huawei,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.260
and basically anyone else who wanted in on the&nbsp;
smartphone market. Meanwhile, from Apple’s side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:37.860
there wasn’t even phones with different screen&nbsp;
sizes up until the iPhone 6. You basically had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:43.320
to choose between a white iPhone and a black&nbsp;
iPhone and that was pretty much it. Also, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:49.020
abundance of choice or lack thereof extended well&nbsp;
beyond the brand of the phone. With Androids, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:53.400
could take advantage of widgets, swap batteries&nbsp;
in a matter of seconds, and add storage like it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:59.280
was nothing. And let’s not forget about how easy&nbsp;
it was to jailbreak them. Jailbreaking or enabling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.200
root access to an Android phone was literally&nbsp;
a matter of pressing a button. With iPhones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:09.780
on the other hand, jailbreaking was a long and&nbsp;
tense process as you hoped and prayed that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:15.540
didn’t brick your phone. But anyway, putting my&nbsp;
childhood fears to the side, when you combine the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:20.220
price, performance, and customizability&nbsp;
of Androids, it’s no wonder why Android&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:29.460
became so popular. But, clearly, these factors&nbsp;
aren’t holding up in 2023, so what happened?

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:36.780
THE CASE FOR IPHONE:&nbsp;

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:41.940
Looking back, I don’t think any of Android’s&nbsp;
winning factors have necessarily fallen out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:48.180
favor. People still want price, performance,&nbsp;
and control but then why is Android getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:53.760
destroyed? Well, I would argue that Android no&nbsp;
longer offers any of these while Apple has made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:07:00.120
strides in all of these. Let’s start with price&nbsp;
for example. Everyone loves to talk about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:06.540
iPhones have become far more expensive but have&nbsp;
you looked at the Android market? The original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:13.140
Samsung Galaxy S1 was launched for $400 in 2010&nbsp;
alongside the iPhone 4 which launched for $600.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:20.400
So, the Samsung was a solid 33% cheaper. But&nbsp;
since then, the price of the Galaxy has not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:26.040
only matched the price of the iPhone or even&nbsp;
the pro iPhone. It has literally overtaken the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:33.480
pro max iPhone as the Galaxy S20 Plus started at&nbsp;
$1200. And it’s not just Samsung who has jacked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:39.660
up prices. Android prices have exploded to match&nbsp;
iPhone prices across the board as everyone tries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:46.200
to be seen as the premium smartphone premium.&nbsp;
Even the Google Pixel 7a which is supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:51.960
be the budget phone now starts at $500. Now of&nbsp;
course, you could argue that the price of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:57.360
iPhone has also skyrocketed during this time&nbsp;
period and that’s completely true, but there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:04.200
one major difference. By jacking up prices, Apple&nbsp;
wasn’t cannabilizing their own userbase. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:08.880
they were just building up the status and appeal&nbsp;
of their phone which is why a lot of people buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:14.100
their phones in the first place. Android on the&nbsp;
other hand was cannabilizing their userbase.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:18.660
When an Android user sees that the new galaxy&nbsp;
is more expensive that the flagship iPhone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:26.760
most don’t say “Wow, Samsung has really stepped up&nbsp;
their quality and status.” Instead, they go “Damn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:31.740
if it’s more expensive than the iPhone,&nbsp;
I might as well just get the iPhone.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:36.480
This has only been exacerbated by Apple becoming&nbsp;
a lot friendlier when it comes to pricing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:42.000
All we hear about Apple is their flagship&nbsp;
costing more than ever but here’s the deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:47.820
the flagships aren’t meant for the average person.&nbsp;
They're meant for the people who want the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:54.000
regardless of price and to shape the Apple brand&nbsp;
image. For everyone else, Apple has actually been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.820
offering cheaper phones than ever starting at&nbsp;
just $430 and going up in increments of $100.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:04.500
So, as Androids have become more expensive&nbsp;
and entry level iPhones have become cheaper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:10.200
pricing has become a non factor when choosing&nbsp;
between iPhone and Android especially when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:16.740
throw in carrier deals that largely favor iPhones.&nbsp;
But, it’s not just pricing that Android has lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:22.800
out on, they’ve also lost out on performance. A&nbsp;
lot of Android phones still have better batteries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:28.200
and better processors and more RAM and all&nbsp;
of that stuff but this no longer translates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:34.020
into real world performance. You see, Apple has&nbsp;
gotten extremely good at optimizing their software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:39.780
to work in synergy with their hardware. So, even&nbsp;
though they still often have worse specs on paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:46.560
they actually end up having better performance and&nbsp;
battery life. Take the Samsung Galaxy S23 and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:53.700
iPhone 14 Pro Max for example. On Geekbench, the&nbsp;
S23 Ultra comes in at 1877 and 4968 ultra while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.700 --> 00:10:01.800
the 14 pro max comes in at 2516 and 6351. In fact,&nbsp;
the top 20 scoring mobile devices all come from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:10.260
Apple but honestly, this is completely irrelevant&nbsp;
because in 2023, performance itself is irrelevant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:15.360
And that’s not to say that people don’t&nbsp;
care about performance but rather that all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:21.060
reputable smartphones are more than capable.&nbsp;
So, performance has also become a non factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:26.460
which brings us into the last hero factor for&nbsp;
Android which was customizability. To be blunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:32.820
Android’s customizability has fallen off a cliff.&nbsp;
They’ve very much taken the Apple approach when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:38.280
it comes to customizing the hardware. You can no&nbsp;
longer switch batteries or upgrade storage or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:44.160
plug in headphones. Even getting a charging brick&nbsp;
is an anomaly nowadays. These android phonemakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:50.040
think that this makes them more premium and&nbsp;
more like Apple but really it just makes them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:56.160
less appealing. And as for the software side of&nbsp;
things, Android is indeed still customizable but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:02.160
so is iPhone. After resisting for years, Apple&nbsp;
has finally caved and given users customization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:07.500
options whether that’s dark mode or widgets or&nbsp;
different fonts or whatever else. This has not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:12.720
only completely eliminated the need to jailbreak&nbsp;
but also the last remaining advantage of Android.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:18.180
But honestly while these factors offer&nbsp;
a logical explanation for the downfall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:23.040
of Android, I think there’s also a&nbsp;
more qualitative explanation at play.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:26.280
WHAT REALLY HAPPENED:&nbsp;

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:34.380
As you can see, Android has inarguably lost&nbsp;
much of it’s distinguishing appeal over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:39.720
past decade but I think there’s more to&nbsp;
this trend than just objective reasoning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:45.000
The X factor that we have yet to discuss&nbsp;
is the rising importance of smartphones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:51.720
Back in the early 2010s, smartphones were the new&nbsp;
tool. It helped you reply to emails and surf the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:57.540
web and chat more conveniently than ever. Most&nbsp;
were looking for the cheapest and most practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:02.940
way to accomplish these tasks and this set the&nbsp;
context for their purchasing decision. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:08.100
weren’t as concerned about small bugs or having&nbsp;
the perfect UI or the smartphones experience or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:13.500
all of these other factors that Apple focused on.&nbsp;
These were seen as nice to haves or luxuries but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:20.340
by no means necessities and as such, the majority&nbsp;
went with Android. But the role of the smartphone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:27.420
within society has largely transformed over the&nbsp;
past 10 years. Smartphones are no longer a tool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:33.360
they are a necessity to engage with the modern&nbsp;
society and economy. As such, people are more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:37.260
willing to invest in a nice smartphone instead&nbsp;
trying to get the cheapest one the market.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:43.620
Moreover, people value the experience part of it&nbsp;
more than ever. They enjoy how seamless iMessage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:49.200
and Facetime, and now even phone calls are on&nbsp;
the iPhone. They appreciate that all of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:53.520
favorite apps are natively built for the&nbsp;
iPhone even if they don’t know that’s why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:58.440
iPhone apps feel better. They love the synergy&nbsp;
between their smartphone and all of their other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:04.200
Apple tech gadgets. And most importantly, they&nbsp;
value fitting in with their friends and family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:09.900
Combine this with the fact that Androids are now&nbsp;
just as expensive, lost their performance lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:15.720
and come along with the same shortfalls as Apple,&nbsp;
and there’s really no reason not to switch to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:22.380
iPhone. And looking forward, this trend will&nbsp;
only amplify as younger generations value these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:31.140
characteristics more than ever. And that’s why&nbsp;
everyone is ditching Android. I would also argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:36.420
that the modern tech economy is destroying the&nbsp;
middle class. Check out this video to learn more.

